# REGRESSION — an `on error:` handler's uses are live from its
# REGISTRATION to function exit, so every statement AFTER the registration
# must see them. A first fix unioned the handler at the registration
# point, which repaired only the statements BEFORE it -- a reverse walk
# visits the later statements first, so the uses must be SEEDED before the
# block walk begins.
#
# The liveness walker is UNDER-APPROXIMATION-CRITICAL: a use it cannot see
# makes a variable look dead at an earlier consuming position, so the value is
# MOVED instead of cloned and the later read finds freed memory. Every cell
# below was rc 0 printing GARBAGE on BOTH backends at the commit named, with
# ggdef printing the right answer wherever it can adjudicate.
#
# Found by R45 Track D brief-review pass 15; RED-verified by the orchestrator
# against the pre-fix compiler.
#
# ⚠ The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into the
# vector. Without it the moved-from read still finds live memory and prints
# correctly — a fixture that proves nothing.
# PROBE A — a name USED IN THE HANDLER is consumed by a statement AFTER the
# handler's registration point. `on error:` runs on error EXIT, which happens
# at some point AFTER the registration, so `s` must be live from the `on
# error:`
# statement to the end of the function.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into the
# vector.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int f(int n) throws String:
    String s = mk("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
    on error:
        print(s)
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(s)
    v.set(0, mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
    if n > 0:
        throw "boom"
    return 1

void main():
    int r = f(1)! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
